    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Energy",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7937\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to authorize NYSERDA to expend proceeds collected from the\nauction at sale of carbon dioxide emission allowances\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo authorize NYSERDA to expend proceeds collected from the auction at\nsale of carbon dioxide emission allowances.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 authorizes and directs NYSERDA to expend an amount not to\nexceed $100 million from proceeds collected from the auction at sale\nof carbon dioxide emission allowances allocation by the Department of\nEnvironmental Conservation on or before March 31, 2017 for the state\nfiscal year commencing April 1, 2016, pending a final order and\ndetermination by the Public Service Commission. This would provide\nfinancial support for the benefit of the electric system and maintain\nthe viability of certain nuclear power plants that demonstrate the\nlack of financial viability absent additional financial support.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nIn November of 2015, the Entergy Corporation, owners and operators of\nthe FitzPatrick Nuclear Facility, announced that it would shut down\nthe nuclear plant after the reactor runs out of fuel. However the\nfacility is vital to the economic stability of the communities in\nOswego County and central New York, in which the facility is located,\nproviding over 600 jobs for the residents of the area.\n\nThis bill authorizes the New York State Research and Development\nAuthority (NYSERDA) to use Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative (RGGI)\nfunds from the sale of carbon dioxide emission allowances in order to\ntemporarily fund an expedited program to provide financial support for\nthe benefit of the electric system. Specifically, this measure would\nmaintain the viability of certain nuclear power plants that are on the\nverge of closure. In addition, this temporary program would allow\nFitzpatrick to continue to operate until tier 3 of the Clean Energy\nStandard is implemented, which would make Fitzpatrick, as well as the\nrest of the state's nuclear fleet, financially stable and able to\ncontinue operation until each plant's license expires.\n\nThe benefits that our nuclear facilities bring to our State in terms\nof jobs, and clean, high-quality power is without dispute. According\nto the PSC, \"Loss of these facilities in the short-term would\nundermine progress towards meeting the State's clean energy goals.\nIdeally, these facilities should be maintained as viable until they\ncan be replaced with new, long-term clean energy generation facilities\nor supplanted by energy efficiency achievements.\"\n\nIn Oswego, the FitzPatrick facility is a major employer and the effect\nof its closure will be felt by hundreds of hardworking employees and\ntheir families, as well as taxpayers, small businesses and communities\nacross Central New York. To date, the FitzPatrick plant not only\nsupports 600 well-paying jobs, it generates an annual $18 million in\nproperty tax revenue, and has an overall impact of $500 million on the\nlocal economy every year. The loss of the FitzPatrick plant would have\na devastating effect on this region and continued operation of this\nthe plant until its license expires is essential.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
